# AjaxClass Legacy Code Notes

`packages/ajax/src/AjaxClass.js` is the entry labeled "existing JS module" in the repository index. These notes focus only on this file. First, note a few imports at the top of the file so there's no need to guess at dependencies from the package directory when revisiting AjaxClass:

```js
import { axios } from '@bundled-es-modules/axios';
import { LionSingleton } from '@lion/core';
import { jsonPrefixTransformerFactory } from './transformers.js';
```

These three references split the leads into two categories: axios is an external dependency for requests, LionSingleton comes from `@lion/core`, and transformer is in `transformers.js` in the same directory. What can be confirmed so far is the file's declarations and these direct dependencies; the index lists its use as code reading and historical behavior checks, which is no substitute for the actual calling context of AjaxClass.

I originally wanted to record how request failures leave the class and where events exit, but the material available this time doesn't include the relevant code snippets. Don't conflate these with the event exit points of `ChoiceInputMixin`; that's the subject of another page. Later, fill in these two points from the method bodies in AjaxClass, especially where transformer is passed in.

# OverlayController Export History Record

2019.11 In the compatibility closeout, OverlayController public export and polite validation feedback were kept in the same record. At the time, they were only two items requiring verification and should not have shared a conclusion merely because they appeared in the same round of work.

First, split this into three items:

- whether the package entrypoint allows consumers to obtain the OverlayController constructor;
- after obtaining the object, whether show and hide are callable lifecycle actions;
- execute polite validation feedback through the current public entrypoint and record what is actually returned or thrown.

Initially, the controller was not documented as a public consumer-facing interface, nor was it designated as an internal object. Only a partial test context was available, so the fact that the object could be constructed in a test file could not substitute for checking the package entrypoint. Likewise, the absence of recorded validation output only indicates a missing record; it does not indicate that feedback capability is absent.

Two approaches were compared at the time. One was to add public export to the compatibility note first, then fill in the entrypoint evidence; the other was to remove public export and continue treating it as an internal object. Neither was adopted. The first would turn an unchecked entrypoint into a basis for integration before verification, while the second would discard the object this round was actually meant to investigate. Provisionally, keep both names and record the entrypoint, constructor, instance actions, and feedback execution separately.

The lookup path is `packages/overlays/test/LocalOverlayController.test.js`. The file header shows:

```js
import { expect, fixture, html, aTimeout, defineCE, unsafeStatic } from '@open-wc/testing';
import Popper from 'popper.js/dist/esm/popper.min.js';
import { keyCodes } from '../src/utils/key-codes.js';
import { simulateTab } from '../src/utils/simulate-tab.js';
```

This is enough to establish its test adjacency to LocalOverlayController, but the path or import list alone still cannot confirm the package export. If the investigation continues from here, first record the object relationships in the tests, then return to the package entrypoint to verify the entrypoint available to consumers. Interface delegation and validator factory compatibility are outside this decision and still need to be handled separately.
